    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d262">

                <pb facs="0371" n="303" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v01/pg303"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72112/1851a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to President Wilson </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-10-09">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 9, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. President</hi>: I am enclosing you the
                    draft of the note to Great Britain upon which we have been working for so long a
                        time.<anchor n="90" xml:id="d262-f1"/> It is unavoidably long, which, to my
                    mind, takes away a certain measure of strength. At the same time I do not see
                    how it can be well abbreviated. Possibly you will think it better to send it by
                    mail, provided it meets with your approval, rather than by telegraph.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d262-f1" n="90" xml:id="d262-f1-content">
                <p>For the note as sent, see <ref target="frus1915#pg_578">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, p. 578</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
